# Line endings.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which
# checks text files out with CRLF. That is harmless for source Go reads or a
# YAML parser, and fatal for anything with a shebang: `#!/bin/sh\r` makes the
# kernel look for an interpreter literally named `/bin/sh\r`, which does not
# exist. Docker then reports
#
#   exec /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# naming the script, while the thing actually missing is the interpreter. The
# file is present and executable; the error points at the wrong object. That
# misdirection is what makes it cost an hour rather than a minute.
#
# eol=lf is enforced rather than left to `text=auto`, because auto-detection
# normalises in the repository but still honours the platform default on
# checkout. These files run on Linux regardless of where they were cloned.
#
# The Dockerfile strips CR from the entrypoint as a second layer, for a user
# whose local git config overrides this file. Belt and braces: this is the
# correct fix, that one is the one that survives a misconfigured clone.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, so intent survives someone loosening the wildcard above.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.env       text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never normalised. A woff2 or a PNG that has been
# through line-ending conversion is corrupt, and silently so.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.db     binary
